Stop selecting a worker once it loses readiness, selector membership,
termination, or a verifiable current state, and make that withdrawal identity-correct,
reversible, and honest about what a removal actually is.

THE DEFECT, in five parts that hide each other. The Kubernetes watcher watches all Pods
and applies the label selector in a client-side filter
(sgl-model-gateway/src/service_discovery.rs): the stream was consumed through
applied_objects() and then passed through a filter_map that dropped any Pod not
matching the selector. A Pod that loses the eligibility label therefore never reached
the handler at all -- a filter can only discard objects, and a withdrawal is exactly the
case where the object is still there. Second, the same filter_map discarded the Delete
event and the Init and InitDone arms of the relist. What applied_objects() actually
does is decode an Event into the object it carries and, with emit_deleted false, pass
Apply and InitApply through while discarding Delete, Init and InitDone: upstream
therefore still saw each object of a relist, and never saw the point at which the
watcher states that an object previously Applied is now absent, which is why an abruptly
removed worker stayed registered for the life of the process. Third, handle_pod_event
had a branch for a healthy Pod and no branch at all for an unhealthy one, and the
deletion-timestamp branch that upstream did have was reachable only after a PodInfo had
been rebuilt from the current object.

Behind all three sat a fourth problem that made a partial fix useless rather than wrong.
The tracker was a HashSet<PodInfo> and PodInfo derives PartialEq, Eq and Hash over the
whole struct, including is_ready, status and ip. A Ready -> NotReady transition, a phase
change or an address change all produce a PodInfo that no longer compares equal to the
registered one, so any removal looked up by the current PodInfo would MISS the live entry
and the worker would stay in the pool; and because the address is part of the key, a
same-name replacement that reused an address aliased the previous identity.

A fifth problem was specific to this gateway. handle_pod_deletion removed the tracking
entry BEFORE submitting the removal job, so a rejected submission, or a job queue that
was not initialized, left the registration forgotten while the worker stayed in the pool:
the gateway reported a withdrawal that never happened and would not retry it.

THE FIX. The watch stream is consumed as raw Events and the selector decision moved into
the handler, where the Pod itself is in hand: a Pod that is terminating, no longer
matches the selectors, or is present but not healthy is a withdrawal. Termination is
checked FIRST, before any status is parsed and before selector membership, because a
terminating Pod can still report Ready=True and Running, and reading health first would
re-register it and leave the withdrawal to the final Delete event -- the preStop path
this replaces. Apply and Delete are handled directly, and the relist is implemented as
the contract describes: Init opens a set, InitApply records UIDs, InitDone withdraws
every tracked UID the listing did not contain.

The tracker becomes a HashMap keyed on the Pod UID, and the stored value is the PodInfo
the worker was REGISTERED with rather than as it is now, so a withdrawal issued for an
old identity removes that old URL and role. A UID-keyed map alone does not make that
true at the registry, because the registry is keyed by URL: if a new UID were admitted at
an address the old UID still held, the map would carry both, and a later Delete for the
old UID would remove the CURRENT occupant from the pool. register_worker therefore
reconciles address ownership before admitting a new UID -- any other UID registered at
the same address is withdrawn first, and a failed withdrawal holds the new add -- so an
address has exactly one owner and a late delete for a replaced UID finds nothing to act
on.

Submitting the removal before the addition is NOT on its own an ordering guarantee, and
this patch does not pretend otherwise. The job queue dispatches each item to its own task
under a global concurrency limit, so two jobs for the same URL can run at the same time
and can finish in the opposite order to the one they were submitted in. Two real races
follow. An addition accepted earlier can still be running when a removal for the same URL
completes, and it can then land afterwards and put a withdrawn worker back into the pool.
And a replacement's addition can complete while the previous owner's removal is still
pending, so the removal then takes out the new worker. This patch therefore carries a
second, narrow hunk into the pinned sgl-model-gateway/src/core/job_queue.rs, for two
reasons. First, the queue gains a LANE per worker URL: a job enters its URL's lane in
submission order and leaves it only when it has finished, so two jobs for one URL can never
overlap and can never finish in the opposite order to the one they were submitted in. The
dispatcher decides a lane and moves straight to the next receive: it never waits on one,
and the concurrency permit is taken inside the lane's own task rather than in the
dispatcher, so a slow worker cannot hold up any other worker even while permits are free.
Idle lanes are dropped by the existing status cleanup task, so the lane map does not grow
for the life of the process. Second, a caller can now ask for a job's real outcome rather
than its acceptance, and withdraw_registration does: it keeps the old registered identity
until the removal job has actually completed, and reports failure -- leaving that identity
in place for the relist-driven retry -- when the job fails or does not finish inside its
bound. Note that the queue's existing status map is keyed by worker URL, so two jobs for
one URL overwrite each other's status there; it cannot be used to tell these jobs apart and
is not used for that here. A Pod whose UID is absent OR EMPTY is refused rather than
admitted, because an empty string is not an identity: it is the one value every
unidentified Pod shares.

An addition is accepted ASYNCHRONOUSLY, so the queue accepting it says nothing about the
worker being in the registry, and the tracker entry is written on acceptance. That makes a
tracked entry and a registered worker two different facts, which upstream did not have to
face because it also wrote the entry on acceptance. The consequence is concrete: a failed
addition left a tracker entry and no worker, and the early return for an already-tracked
identity then swallowed every later event for that Pod -- including the relist's -- so
the worker was never added for the rest of the process. The early return is therefore
taken only when the queue has no failure recorded for that URL, which it cannot have when
the addition ran, because the queue removes a status on success and records it on error.
When there is such a failure the addition is RE-ISSUED under the same UID: the identity is
what a later withdrawal matches against and must not be changed to work around a failure,
and the recorded address is re-withdrawn first so a partially applied addition cannot
survive the retry. The same event also drives the relist, so a failed addition converges
without a new timer. A failure is not fabricated when there is no queue to have failed,
because in that case no addition was ever delivered either.

A Pod that still matches the selectors but whose current status or address cannot be read
is WITHDRAWN, not retained. Eligibility is not health, and a registration that cannot be
shown healthy must not stay selectable.

withdraw_registration now returns whether the removal job COMPLETED, and the tracking
entry is dropped only when it returns true. A rejected submission, an uninitialized
queue, a failing removal job, or one that outruns its bound all retain the registration,
log the first error, and let the retry deliver it instead of reporting a withdrawal that
never happened. A true result is still weaker than the worker having stopped serving: the
job reports the outcome of its own workflow against the registry and nothing here observes
a request afterwards. The drop is additionally guarded
on the exact PodInfo, so a concurrent update for the same UID is not silently discarded.
A true result here is strictly weaker than the worker having stopped serving: this
submits a job and does not observe the registry afterwards. Confirming that a worker is
no longer selected is a separate, identity-bound observation and is NOT claimed by this
patch.

The watcher loop treats a false as a reason to end the stream, so the existing backoff
recreates the watcher and the relist that comes with it reconciles whatever is still
held. That is the only retry added here: no second timer, no polling loop, and a failed
withdrawal is not left waiting for a Pod event that may never arrive. The backoff is
taken alone on that path rather than followed by the full check-interval sleep, which
would push the retry past any sensible withdrawal budget.

P/D behaviour is preserved: the worker_type and bootstrap_port derivation and the whole
WorkerConfigRequest shape are unchanged, and the prefill/decode/regular routing reaches
them through the new decision points.

AGAINST: sgl-project/sglang, tag gateway-v0.3.1 (commit
404329ae730680f9a5c0fb18f17b5dbc74d77f6f), the pin named by SGLANG_GATEWAY_VERSION in
pack/llm-router/Dockerfile. The patch carries repository-root-relative paths and the
image applies it from the clone root, which is the form those paths imply; --directory is
deliberately not used, because it would prefix the already-root-relative paths a second
time. The stage then greps for a symbol this patch introduces, so a patch skipped for
path reasons is a build failure rather than an unpatched build reporting success. Both
properties were checked against a pristine clone of the pinned tree: the apply exits 0
and changes the file, while the same patch against a different revision of the same file
fails loudly and changes nothing.

Upstream ships no Cargo.lock for this crate, so the build resolves its dependency graph
afresh. That asymmetry against the vLLM router stage is deliberate and is measured rather
than papered over: the build log and the resolved dependency set are recorded as
evidence, and no byte-reproducibility is claimed.

The added tests drive the discovery decisions through handle_watch_event and assert on
the tracker and on the returned delivery flag: readiness loss, selector loss,
termination while reporting Ready, termination with no status, and an eligible Pod whose
current status is unusable all reach the withdrawal path; a relist targets the absent
identity; an address takeover is held while the previous owner's withdrawal cannot be
delivered, and the new UID is not admitted; a late delete for an untracked UID cannot
reach the current owner; an absent or empty UID is refused; and a removal that was not
queued reports failure and retains the registration for retry.

The delivered-removal leg is not asserted in-crate, for a reason that is a property of
the fixture rather than of the fix: the gateway's test AppContext deliberately leaves the
job queue uninitialized so tests do not spawn background workers, so a removal can never
be observed as delivered there. The queue patch adds no test of its own for the same
reason -- with the queue uninitialized there is no completion to observe, and a test
asserting only refusal would prove nothing about ordering. The failed-addition retry has
the same shape and is not asserted in-crate for the same reason: it is decided by whether
the queue recorded a failure for a URL, which an uninitialized queue never can, and
asserting the early return in that fixture would pass whether or not the retry exists.
Ordering, delayed completion, completion-failure and the failed-addition retry are
therefore measured against the built binary on placement evidence in the operator's
event-matrix harness, on real request markers and Pod-UID arrival joins, rather than
claimed from a unit test here.

